course: dictionary
lesson: 3587
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Read the words around the word: they are its clues. «The shin bone, the tibia, lies just under the skin with little padding, which is why kicks hurt and footballers wear shin guards; the thin fibula runs beside it.» Here the tibia is a bone, so the fibula is a bone too, one that runs beside the shin.
explain: Now read a different text. «The museum case held a bronze fibula, found by archaeologists.» There is no leg or ankle nearby, and the words case and museum point to something you can hold. A fibula can be a pin used to fasten clothes. Always ask: which meaning fits the words around it? Then try that meaning in the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Scientists and historians use the same word for two different things, so the clues matter. In science, the fibula is a bone. In history, it is an ancient pin. If you swap the meaning into the sentence and it sounds silly, try the other meaning.
fact: The fibula is the thinner of the two long bones in the lower leg, on the outside.
fact: The tibia is the larger shin bone, and the fibula runs beside it.
fact: A fibula is also an ancient brooch or pin used to fasten clothes, as worn by Romans and Celts.
fact: The plural of fibula can be fibulae or fibulas.
fact: The fibula helps support the ankle.
exercise: «The shin bone, the tibia, lies just under the skin.» Which bone runs beside the tibia? | *Fibula | Femur | Humerus | Radius
exercise: Archaeologists find a Roman «fibula» in the soil. What is it? | *An ancient pin or brooch used to fasten clothes | A type of football boot | A bone in the lower leg | A small Roman coin
exercise: What does the phrase «tibia and fibula» name? | Two Roman pins | The two sides of a football pitch | The two bones of the upper arm | *The two long bones in the lower leg
test: «He broke his fibula playing football, so his lower leg had to heal.» Which part of the body is hurt? | The upper arm | *The lower leg | The back | The head
test: Why might a doctor care about the fibula near the ankle? | It is a pin found in a museum | It keeps clothes fastened | It stops the knee from bending | *It helps support the ankle
exam: In «The museum case held a bronze fibula», which meaning of fibula fits best? | *An ancient pin used to fasten clothes | A bone in the leg | A type of shoe | A bone in the arm
